> The compatibility I think of is that of the personal nethack configuration.

Compatibility is no asset per se; a bad user interfaces should be fixed
instead of be kept "compatible".

> Neither change given above required a change of the config file for any
> of the players that liked to ignore the new feature.

I mentioned it upthread; YMMV here, but picking up _everything_ is *not* a
sensible setting with all the loot lying around in the dungeons. You should
either pick up selected types, or not pick up anything by configuration and
then select the specific objects that you get through the menu; or you will
die quickly. Of course I can't rule out that there are a few beginners who
define this option that way, to pick up everything, but such a non-sensible
default behaviour should certainly not be supported by a sensible user
interface.

>>> All solutions as well satisfying autopickup exceptions as well I could
>>> think of are ugly.
>>
>> Fixing the
>> incoherent interface, OTOH, would not introduce any bad behaviour. (YMMV.)
>> What is it that you are missing and think would produce ugly solutions?
> 
> A simple change in the semantics of an empty pickup_types would require all
> players still using the old semantics to change their personal configuration.

I think a sensible solution would and could distinguish the case of a
non-existing (or equivalently; a commented out) config file attribute.
So even if one thinks it would make sense to keep the shortcut for a
non-sensible 'pickup-all' as initial 'unset' semantics, as soon as you
assign anything explicitly only those types (none, if empty assignment)
should be considered.

  # pickup-types=        ## commented out or unset: pickup all (if you like)
  pickup-types="?!=/     ## the selected types (and the exceptions)
  pickup-types=          ## only and the exceptions

> That the one ugly way. The other ugly ways show up when trying to avoid the
> first one, e.g. by using a special pattern for specifying nothing to pickup
> (besides there already is a fine one: "]").

Using this "]" is an interesting work-around! - Before you posted I thought
of using "_" for that purpose, or a boulder, an object that you cannot pick
up. - But it's only a work-around. It wouldn't protect from the bad surprise
that I described in my original posting of this thread; and user interfaces
should conform to the "Least Surprise" paradigm.

